    "category": "120V, 12A, Internal Air",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "The Hobart AirForce 12ci is for occasional light cutting where you do not own, or do not want to drag around, an air compressor, because it has one built in. Tractor Supply's listing gives a clean cut of 1/8-inch steel and a maximum thickness of 1/4 inch, a 35 percent duty cycle at 104°F, and 120-volt input. A buyer answer on the same listing notes that the 1/4-inch figure is a sever cut and that 1/4-inch pipe is slow and difficult, which is a fair description of what 12 amps can do. Treat it as a sheet-metal and light-gauge tool for auto body panels, signs and thin brackets. If you already own a compressor that can supply 4 to 6 cfm, a 30-amp machine with external air will cut far more for not much more money.",
    "pros": [
      "Built-in air compressor, no hose needed",
      "Runs on 120V household power",
      "Clean cuts on 1/8-inch steel, per listing",
      "Simple, portable setup",
      "Brand-name support"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Only 12 amps; 1/4 inch is a slow sever cut",
      "Limited to sheet metal and light gauge",
      "Less capacity per dollar than external-air units"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Buy the AirForce 12ci only for occasional thin-metal cutting without a compressor; anyone cutting 1/4 inch regularly needs a 30-amp machine."
  },
  {
    "id": "yeswelder-cut-55ds",
    "name": "YesWelder CUT-55DS",
    "badge": "Best Budget",
    "category": "110/220V, 55A, External Air",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "The YesWelder CUT-55DS is the low-cost way to get a dual-voltage plasma cutter with a non-touch pilot arc. WeldGuru's spec table lists 10 to 55 amps on 220 volts and 10 to 35 amps on 110 volts, a duty cycle of 60 percent at 55 amps on 220 volts, a rated clean cut of 1/2 inch and a maximum sever of 3/4 inch. The pilot arc uses high-frequency start, so the torch does not need to touch the work, and the box includes a 13-foot torch, ground clamp, consumables and an air filter, with a one-year warranty. Treat the cut ratings with caution: one buyer on a deal forum said a similar unit barely managed 1/2 inch with a rough edge and advised planning on half the claimed capacity, and a CNC forum user found the cut chart inconsistent. High-frequency start can also interfere with nearby electronics and CNC controllers.",
    "pros": [
      "Low price for 55 amps and dual voltage",
      "Non-touch pilot arc start",
      "60 percent duty cycle at 55A, per WeldGuru",
      "Includes torch, consumables and air filter",
      "Rated 1/2-inch clean cut on paper"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Owners report real cuts below the rating",
      "High-frequency start can disturb electronics",
      "One-year warranty and thinner support"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "For a budget hobby cutter, the CUT-55DS is good value; size it on the assumption it cuts closer to 1/4 to 3/8 inch cleanly, not 1/2."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Plasma Cutters",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-plasma-cutter/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What size plasma cutter do I need?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Match the rated cut to the thickness you cut most often. A 30-amp unit like the Hypertherm Powermax30 XP is rated for 3/8 inch, and a 40 to 45-amp unit for about 5/8 inch per listings. Severance figures are higher but leave rough, slow cuts."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do plasma cutters need an air compressor?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most do. Listings give about 4 scfm at 80 psi for 30-amp Hypertherm units and about 6 scfm at 90 psi for 40 to 45-amp units. Some light-duty machines like the Hobart AirForce 12ci have a built-in compressor but much less cutting power."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a plasma cutter run on 120 volts?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, many are dual voltage, but output drops on household power. Hobart lists the AirForce 40i at about 20 to 27 amps on 120-volt circuits versus 40 amps on 240 volts, so cut capacity is lower on 120 volts."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a plasma cutter cut aluminum and stainless?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Plasma cuts any conductive metal, including stainless steel and aluminum, which an oxy-fuel torch handles poorly. Capacities on aluminum are typically lower than on mild steel, so check the manufacturer's rating."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is a pilot arc on a plasma cutter?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A pilot arc starts the plasma without touching the torch to the work. It extends consumable life and makes it easier to cut painted, rusty or expanded metal. Some budget units use high-frequency start, which can interfere with nearby electronics."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long do plasma cutter consumables last?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It depends on amperage, air quality, piercing and technique. Clean, dry air and proper settings extend life, while piercing thick steel and wet air shorten it. Budget for regular electrode and nozzle replacements and buy a brand whose parts you can find locally."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a plasma cutter worth it for a home shop?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "If you cut steel more than occasionally, especially shapes or stainless and aluminum, a plasma cutter is much faster and cleaner than a grinder. For a few straight cuts a year, an angle grinder with a cutoff wheel is cheaper and needs no compressor."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Tools & Equipment",
    tags: [
      "best plasma cutter",
      "plasma cutter",
      "best plasma cutter for beginners",
      "plasma cutter with built in compressor"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated October 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Best Plasma Cutters <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">From Hobby to Shop</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          A plasma cutter slices steel, stainless and aluminum faster and cleaner than a grinder or torch. These six cover hobby, farm and fabrication work.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#hypertherm-powermax30-xp"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Jump to the Picks
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="5.1k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#how-we-picked" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. How We Picked</a>
          <a href="#rated-vs-sever" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Rated Cut vs Severance</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 6 Picks</a>
          <a href="#air" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Air Supply and Compressors</a>
          <a href="#power" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. 120V, 240V and Duty Cycle</a>
          <a href="#pilot-arc" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Pilot Arc, Torch and Consumables</a>
          <a href="#plasma-vs" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Plasma vs Grinder vs Torch</a>
          <a href="#safety" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Safety Basics</a>
          <a href="#cnc" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Hand Cutting vs CNC Tables</a>
          <a href="#running-cost" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Running Cost and Upkeep</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">12. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The best plasma cutter is sized by its rated cut, not the bigger severance number printed on the box, and it is only as good as the air you feed it. Buy for the thickness you cut most often, make sure your compressor can keep up, and pay for a brand with consumables you can actually buy. Those three choices matter more than peak amperage.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not test these plasma cutters in a lab. We compared manufacturer specification sheets, retailer listings and independent reviews, and credited each number to its source. Plasma ratings are among the most inconsistent in the tool world, so where listings disagreed on cut capacity, air demand, duty cycle or warranty, we flagged the conflict rather than choosing the best-sounding figure.
          </p>

          <p>
            We weighted the rated or recommended cut, the thickness a machine cuts cleanly at a usable speed, above the severance figure. We also weighted dual-voltage input for home shops, realistic air demand, and the availability of consumables, because electrodes and nozzles wear out and a cutter you cannot get parts for becomes scrap.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hypertherm, Hobart and Miller fill most of the list because they publish clearer specifications and have dealer networks for parts and service. We kept one import unit for buyers on a tight budget and one built-in-compressor unit for people who do not own a compressor, with honest notes on what each gives up.
          </p>

          <p>
            We did not include industrial three-phase machines. Every pick here runs on single-phase power available in a home garage or farm shop, either 120 volts, 240 volts or both.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="rated-vs-sever" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Rated Cut vs Severance: The Number That Matters
          </h2>

          <p>
            Plasma cutters list two or three thickness figures, and the biggest one is the least useful. The rated or recommended cut is the thickness the machine cuts cleanly at a practical travel speed, usually defined by manufacturers as around 10 inches per minute. Hypertherm, for example, lists a 3/8-inch recommended cut for the Powermax30 XP at 20 inches per minute.
          </p>

          <p>
            The severance capacity is the thickest material the machine can get through at all, slowly, with a rough edge and heavy dross. Hypertherm lists 5/8-inch severance for the same Powermax30 XP. Severance is useful for an occasional emergency cut, not for daily work.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some brands, especially budget imports, advertise a clean cut figure that is closer to other makers' severance numbers. That is why the YesWelder's 1/2-inch rating and the Hypertherm's 3/8-inch rating are not directly comparable, and why owners report cutting less than the box suggests.
          </p>

          <p>
            Size your cutter so the thickness you cut most often sits at or below its rated cut. If you regularly cut 3/8-inch steel, a 30-amp machine rated at 3/8 is the floor and a 40 to 45-amp machine gives you headroom and faster, cleaner edges.
          </p>

          <p>
            A quick way to compare machines is to look at amperage. In general, 30-amp units handle about 3/8 inch cleanly, 40 to 45-amp units around 1/2 to 5/8 inch and 60-amp units about 3/4 inch, according to manufacturer ratings. Use that as a sanity check against any figure that looks too good for the amperage.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Our 6 Picks
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not lab-test these plasma cutters. The picks are researched from manufacturer specification sheets, retailer listings and independent reviews, and each figure is credited to its source. Where sources disagreed, we say so on the card.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <div id={product.id} class="scroll-mt-24 mt-12 first:mt-8">
              <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-sm hover:shadow-md transition-shadow">
                <div class="bg-gray-900 px-6 py-4 flex items-center justify-between">
                  <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
                    <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black">
                      {index + 1}
                    </span>
                    {productImages[product.id] && (
                      <img
                        src={productImages[product.id]}
                        alt={product.name}
                        loading="lazy"
                        width="40"
                        height="40"
                        class="w-10 h-10 rounded-lg bg-white object-contain p-1 shrink-0"
                      />
                    )}
                    <div>
                      <h3 class="text-white font-black text-lg leading-snug m-0"><span class="block text-[#38BDF8] text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wide mb-1 sm:hidden">{product.badge}</span>{product.name}</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs">{product.category}</p>
                    </div>
