        <div id="air" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Air Supply and Compressors
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most plasma cutters use compressed air as the plasma gas, and air is where many home setups fall short. Listings give about 4 scfm at 80 psi for the Powermax30 XP and about 6 scfm at 90 psi for the Powermax45 XP and Hobart AirForce 40i. If your compressor cannot deliver that flow continuously, the cut quality drops and consumables wear faster.
          </p>

          <p>
            Small pancake compressors are rated at low flow and run constantly trying to keep up. For a 30 to 45-amp plasma cutter, a compressor with a larger tank and a flow rating above the cutter's requirement is the realistic minimum. Check the compressor's scfm at 90 psi, not its peak or open-flow number.
          </p>

          <p>
            Air quality matters as much as quantity. Water and oil in the air line shorten electrode and nozzle life and cause erratic arcs. Hypertherm calls for clean, dry, oil-free air, so add a filter and moisture separator at the cutter and drain the compressor tank regularly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Built-in-compressor machines like the Hobart AirForce 12ci avoid all of that, but the small onboard pump limits power. They suit thin sheet metal and occasional use, not regular 1/4-inch work.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hose length and fittings also cost pressure. A long, narrow air hose between the compressor and the cutter drops pressure at the torch, so use the hose size the manual recommends and keep the run reasonable. Quick-connect fittings that restrict flow are a common, hidden cause of weak cuts.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="power" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. 120V, 240V and Duty Cycle
          </h2>

          <p>
            Dual-voltage plasma cutters can run on household 120-volt power at reduced output and full output on 240 volts. Hobart's sheet for the AirForce 40i shows how much the input matters: about 20 amps on a 120-volt 15-amp circuit, 27 amps on a 120-volt 20-amp circuit and 40 amps on 240 volts. Cutting capacity scales with that output.
          </p>

          <p>
            Duty cycle is the share of a ten-minute period a cutter can run at a given output before cooling. Hypertherm lists the Powermax30 XP at 35 percent on 240 volts and 20 percent on 120 volts. Plasma cuts are usually short, so duty cycle matters most on long straight cuts and production work.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a home shop, plan on a 240-volt circuit if you will cut 3/8-inch steel and thicker regularly. A dryer-style 240-volt outlet or a dedicated welder circuit works, and adding one is a job for a licensed electrician.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plasma cutters can run on generators, but inverter machines prefer clean power. Size the generator for the input draw at the output you use, and check the manual's minimum generator size before relying on it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the plug before you buy. Some machines ship with a 240-volt plug and adapters, others with a 120-volt plug only, and adapters vary by market. Hypertherm lists plug adapters for 120-volt 15-amp and 240-volt 20-amp circuits on CSA models of the Powermax30 XP, which makes moving between outlets simple.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pilot-arc" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Pilot Arc, Torch and Consumables
          </h2>

          <p>
            A pilot arc starts the plasma without touching the torch to the metal, which extends consumable life and lets you cut through paint, rust and expanded metal. Most modern units on this list use a pilot arc. Some budget machines use high-frequency start, which works but can interfere with nearby electronics and CNC controllers.
          </p>

          <p>
            The consumables, electrode, nozzle, swirl ring and shield, wear with every start and every cut. Their cost and availability decide your long-term running cost more than the purchase price. Hypertherm, Hobart and Miller consumables are stocked by welding suppliers; budget imports often require ordering online.
          </p>

          <p>
            Drag cutting, resting the shield on the work, is easiest for beginners. Many torches support it with the right consumables. For thicker steel, a standoff guide or a straight-edge improves cut quality and squareness.
          </p>

          <p>
            Torch lead length matters in a shop. Longer leads let you reach a workpiece without moving the machine but cost more. A 15 to 20-foot lead suits most home shops.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep a small parts box with the cutter: spare electrodes, nozzles, a swirl ring and a shield cap. Most cutting problems, from a wandering arc to heavy dross, trace back to a worn nozzle or electrode, and swapping them is a two-minute job.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="plasma-vs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Plasma vs Grinder vs Oxy-Fuel Torch
          </h2>

          <p>
            An angle grinder with a cutoff wheel is the cheapest way to cut steel, and it is still the right tool for occasional straight cuts in thin stock. It is slow on thick steel and on curves, throws sparks and makes heat-tinted edges, but it needs no air or special power.
          </p>

          <p>
            An oxy-fuel torch cuts very thick carbon steel and needs no electricity, which is why it remains common on farms. It cannot cut stainless or aluminum well, the heat-affected zone is larger, and the cylinders add cost and safety concerns.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plasma sits between them. It cuts steel, stainless and aluminum, handles curves and shapes, and leaves a narrow kerf with little heat distortion on thin material. It needs compressed air and electricity, and consumables are an ongoing cost.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a home or farm shop that cuts up to about 1/2-inch steel and some aluminum, plasma is the most versatile option. Keep a grinder for cleanup and bevels; you will still use it on almost every project.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Safety Basics
          </h2>

          <p>
            Plasma cutting produces intense ultraviolet light, sparks, molten metal and fumes. General guidance from the American Welding Society and ANSI Z49.1 is to wear shaded eye protection appropriate for the amperage, commonly a shade 5 to 8 for lower-amperage plasma, along with flame-resistant clothing and leather gloves.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fumes from cutting coated, galvanized or painted metal are hazardous. OSHA guidance stresses ventilation, and cutting galvanized steel releases zinc fumes. Remove coatings where possible and cut in a well-ventilated area or under fume extraction.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sparks can travel far and ignite sawdust, rags and fuel. Clear combustibles from the area, keep a fire extinguisher within reach and watch the area after cutting. Never cut containers that held fuel or chemicals.
          </p>

          <p>
            Follow the manual's grounding instructions, keep cables in good condition, and do not work in wet conditions. Plasma cutters use high open-circuit voltages, so treat the torch and work clamp with respect.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cnc" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Hand Cutting vs CNC Tables
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most buyers cut by hand with a straight-edge or template, and every machine here does that. If you plan to build or buy a CNC plasma table later, look for a machine with a CPC or machine interface port and a mechanized torch option, as offered on Powermax45 XP packages.
          </p>

          <p>
            CNC tables expose the weaknesses of cheaper machines. High-frequency start can disturb controllers, inconsistent arc voltage makes torch height control harder, and cut charts need to be accurate. A CNC forum user noted the YesWelder's chart did not line up with its stated output.
          </p>

          <p>
            CNC cutting also runs longer cuts, so duty cycle and consumable life matter more. A 45-amp machine at a moderate setting will run more comfortably than a 30-amp machine at its limit.
          </p>

          <p>
            If CNC is a real plan, buy the CNC-ready machine now. Retrofitting a hand-only unit is often impossible or unreliable.
          </p>

          <p>
            For hand cutting, simple accessories go a long way. A circle-cutting guide, a magnetic straight-edge and a roller guide turn a freehand torch into a tool that makes repeatable parts, at a fraction of the cost of a CNC table.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="running-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Running Cost and Upkeep
          </h2>

          <p>
            The purchase price is only the start. Electrodes and nozzles are the main running cost, and they wear fastest when you pierce thick steel, cut with wet air or drag the torch at the wrong setting. Buying consumables in kits from a welding supplier is usually cheaper than single parts, and brand-name machines tend to get more cuts from each set because the parts are made to tighter tolerances.
          </p>

          <p>
            Electricity is a small cost by comparison, since plasma cuts are short. The compressor is the bigger load, and a large compressor running for long cutting sessions adds up. Fixing leaks in air lines and fittings saves both power and compressor wear.
          </p>

          <p>
            Basic upkeep keeps a cutter reliable for years. Drain the compressor tank after each session, check the air filter and moisture trap, inspect the torch lead for cuts and burns, and replace consumables in matched sets. Blowing dust out of the machine's vents occasionally helps it run cool and protects the duty cycle.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, store the cutter somewhere dry. Moisture is the enemy of plasma systems, both in the air supply and inside the machine, and a damp shed shortens the life of electronics and consumables alike.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on the severance number</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Severance is the thickest material a machine can barely get through. Size your cutter by its rated or recommended cut, and assume budget machines' clean-cut claims run optimistic. If you cut 3/8-inch steel often, a machine rated at 3/8 is the minimum.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Underpowering the air supply</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A plasma cutter needs steady flow at pressure, often 4 to 6 scfm at 80 to 90 psi. A small pancake compressor cannot keep up, and the result is poor cuts and burned consumables. Check the compressor's scfm at 90 psi before buying either.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the air filter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water and oil in compressed air ruin electrodes and nozzles quickly and cause sputtering arcs. Fit a filter and moisture separator at the cutter and drain the compressor tank after each session.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running at full output on 120 volts</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Dual-voltage machines lose much of their output on household power. Expect thin cuts at reduced speed on 120 volts and plan for a 240-volt circuit if thicker cuts are routine.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Cutting coated or galvanized steel indoors without ventilation</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Coatings and zinc release harmful fumes when cut. Remove coatings where possible, ventilate well and avoid breathing the plume. Never cut containers that held fuel or chemicals.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

