        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size plasma cutter do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Match the rated cut to the thickness you cut most often. A 30-amp unit like the Hypertherm Powermax30 XP is rated for 3/8 inch, and a 40 to 45-amp unit for about 5/8 inch per listings. Severance figures are higher but leave rough, slow cuts.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do plasma cutters need an air compressor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most do. Listings give about 4 scfm at 80 psi for 30-amp Hypertherm units and about 6 scfm at 90 psi for 40 to 45-amp units. Some light-duty machines like the Hobart AirForce 12ci have a built-in compressor but much less cutting power.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a plasma cutter run on 120 volts?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, many are dual voltage, but output drops on household power. Hobart lists the AirForce 40i at about 20 to 27 amps on 120-volt circuits versus 40 amps on 240 volts, so cut capacity is lower on 120 volts.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a plasma cutter cut aluminum and stainless?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Plasma cuts any conductive metal, including stainless steel and aluminum, which an oxy-fuel torch handles poorly. Capacities on aluminum are typically lower than on mild steel, so check the manufacturer's rating.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a pilot arc on a plasma cutter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A pilot arc starts the plasma without touching the torch to the work. It extends consumable life and makes it easier to cut painted, rusty or expanded metal. Some budget units use high-frequency start, which can interfere with nearby electronics.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do plasma cutter consumables last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on amperage, air quality, piercing and technique. Clean, dry air and proper settings extend life, while piercing thick steel and wet air shorten it. Budget for regular electrode and nozzle replacements and buy a brand whose parts you can find locally.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a plasma cutter worth it for a home shop?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If you cut steel more than occasionally, especially shapes or stainless and aluminum, a plasma cutter is much faster and cleaner than a grinder. For a few straight cuts a year, an angle grinder with a cutoff wheel is cheaper and needs no compressor.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

